            <ArticleTitle>Correlation among physical impairments, pain, disability, and patient satisfaction in patients with chronic neck pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>534-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the correlations among pain, physical impairments, disability, and patient satisfaction in patients with chronic neck pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A longitudinal cohort study with 6-month follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Institutional practice.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Subjects (N=218) with chronic neck pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Subjects were treated with different physiotherapy modalities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data were obtained for self-reported disability, verbal numeric pain scale, patient satisfaction, and 2 measures of physical impairments during the initial visit, at 6 weeks, and finally at 6 months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The correlation among 4 sets of measurements varied. Moderate correlation was noted between disability and patient satisfaction ( r range, .50-.65), and between disability and pain ( r range, .55-.63). A fair relationship was found between pain and patient satisfaction ( r range, .43-.48), but only weak relationships were found between physical impairments and pain ( r range, -.08 to -.25). The correlations tended to increase in the follow-up assessments.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">No strong correlations were found among disability, patient satisfaction, pain, and physical impairments although the correlations tended to increase in the follow-up assessments. The findings support the suggestion that clinicians should address as many relevant aspects of a presenting clinical entity as possible in the management of chronic neck pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
